**DISAGREE as written. Put Deck 1 to Alex today, unchanged. The smallest correction is to use it to decide whether to design a larger comparison, and leave the release decision for later.** If Alex spots both planted wrong answers and agrees with his own earlier marks, we learn that he could read these cards consistently. We still need a fair comparison to learn which system gives him better research. [D560](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:7088) · [Deck design](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/pilot-deck-1/DESIGN.md:64)

1. **What to stop.** Pause question-writing changes, the listing census, model trials, and further page, planner, live-store and integration work. The record supports pausing later release engineering while the earlier quality question is open. The page work also addressed a real display defect, so I would distinguish that fix from the live-store expansion. Ask Alex to defer D658 rather than record the pause as decided. He explicitly approved that work, and it is still listed as the next task. [Working rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:375) · [Page defect](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:7179) · [Live-store decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:7735) · [D658](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9208) · [Current state](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/CURRENT_STATE.md:433)

   Correct two claims in the rationale. D656’s share-control rule was written into the test before the send. Keep the recorded failure and the separate finding that the edit recovered all nine selected attempts, while the listing omission appeared in both versions. Also, the stop hook did keep reopening work, but the third rejection led to an inert handoff draft. D623’s fault rates explain why the zero-fault bar was a poor target on those readings. They do not establish astra’s chance of passing it. [Prewritten test rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/narrow-d436/paired_probe.py:13) · [D656](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9167) · [D623](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8382) · [D643](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8803)

2. **What the deck can decide.** The luna-era week is fine for testing whether Alex can make the marks. It cannot assess astra’s output. Use **“enough to design the next comparison”** if he marks the full deck within 45 minutes, rejects both plants, accepts the supported control, and matches both marks on at least two of three repeats. Treat 30 minutes as the target. If any condition is unmet, including a skipped control or “cannot tell” on one, inspect why before relying on the method. A missed plant, a disputed supported card and a slow review may call for different changes. Either way, list the real-card disagreements and Alex’s reasons, record whether he opened the full material, and include the three missing-fact sheets. Use those disagreements to inspect particular judgments. A selected deck cannot establish a reader family’s overall reliability or change its role in a gate. [Deck selection and reporting](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/pilot-deck-1/DESIGN.md:49) · [Repeat and control tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/pilot-deck-1/tally_deck.py:44) · [D661](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9283)

3. **What to ask Alex, in order.**
   - **Today:** “Will you mark the deck, and may we defer the new final-answer reference until we see your marks?” Recommend yes to both. If he wants reference work to continue, ask whether he wants the approved luna reference completed or a separately reviewed design for astra’s questions. [D560](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:7088) · [D658](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9208) · [Astra remeasurement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/reflection-8/LOG.md:49)
   - **After the deck:** “Should the six requirements still block release, or should we report those failures and decide readiness with a controlled comparison of what the analyst receives?” Recommend the second if the pilot supports designing that comparison. The product review supplies evidence for the comparison. A numerical definition of “beats” belongs in that same decision, so options **(b)** and **(c)** should be combined. [D560](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:7100) · [Release checks](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/PRD.md:126)
   - **When the comparison is concrete:** Ask for a reconsideration point. Recommend reviewing direction after the first comparison capable of deciding something, or at an agreed date if no such comparison can be designed. Use real earlier libraries as the working assumption. D606 already adopted that condition provisionally, so raise it with Alex if the design needs a different start. Keep the uncommitted paths as a separate repository decision. [D544](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:6634) · [D606](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8064) · [Commit authority](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:46)

4. **What makes the way back exact.** Put the deck first in the handoff and record what can reopen work. If the marks are pending, report the hold. If they support a comparison design, require both systems to start on the same date with comparable libraries, enough fresh material and reader time to resolve a meaningful difference, and a written rule for what would favor either system or leave the result unclear. If that design cannot clear the earlier feasibility objections, take that finding to Alex. The stop hook needs to accept that hold instead of treating it as a reason to start another task. [Comparison feasibility and correction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/comparison-feasibility/RESULT.md:5) · [D612 reopening conditions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8187) · [D643 stop-hook record](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:8803)
